From f424889e62b02bc41a1fd8dc37401d85c78e86cc Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Hades <15710513+hadesdevls@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Tue, 20 Oct 2026 15:39:16 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] AudioEngine: PCM in no more channels than the display takes

A display says in its EDID which audio it takes, as short audio
descriptors, each with the most channels of its format. Kodi read only
which formats there are (CAEELDParser leaves the channel count of a
descriptor out), and took the channels of an HDMI output from the
speaker allocation, or, where the display has none, from the channel
maps that ALSA offers. For a display without a speaker allocation the
ELD code of the kernel assumes every speaker, "to not prohibit
multi-channel audio playback", and the HDA HDMI codec allows as many
channels of PCM as the descriptor with the most channels, compressed
ones included (snd_hdmi_eld_update_pcm_info()). So a display that takes
linear PCM in 2 channels and Dolby Digital Plus in 8 was sent PCM in up
to 8 channels whenever Kodi decoded a multichannel track.

Seen with an LG C5 and its sound bar on eARC (2026-10-15): on that day
its EDID declared LPCM in 2 channels, AC-3 and E-AC-3, without a
speaker allocation, and a DTS-HD Master Audio track, which the TV does
not take and Kodi decoded, played nothing. On other days the same TV
declared LPCM in 8 channels, and the same track decoded to 7.1 played.

The ELD parser now keeps the most channels of linear PCM that the
display declares (CAEDeviceInfo::m_maxPcmChannels: the largest channel
count of its LPCM descriptors; 2, basic audio, when it has descriptors
and none of LPCM; 0 when it has none, not known), and the engine limits
the "Number of channels" setting to it when it loads its settings: a
display of 2 channels gets 2.0, of 6 gets 5.1. That is the same as the
setting at 2.0, and so is what Kodi does then: a multichannel track is
downmixed to stereo, or, with passthrough and "Enable Dolby Digital
transcoding" on, encoded to Dolby Digital and passed through. The
setting is not changed: the audio data of a display that change are a
device change, which loads the settings again. Passthrough is not
affected. The log says when the channels are limited, once for each
device and setting; the list of devices shows the value.

<audio><pcmchannelsfromeld>false</pcmchannelsfromeld></audio> in
advancedsettings.xml turns the limit off, for a display that takes
more than it says.

The rules are functions of their own (AEPcmChannels.h) with a test
(TestAEPcmChannels), on the descriptors of the EDIDs of the reports:
that LG C5 on both days, its sound bar, and a Hisense TV.
